---
// src/pages/docs/validation.astro
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const validationExample = `
import { createServer } from 'afterlink';
import { z } from 'zod';

// Define expected payload validation schema
const MessageSchema = z.object({
  username: z.string().min(3).max(20),
  message: z.string().min(1).max(500),
  timestamp: z.number().int()
});

const server = createServer({ port: 8080 });

// Register server schema validation for DATA type messages (0x01)
server.registerSchema('DATA', MessageSchema);

server.on('message', (socket, frame) => {
  // If frame reaches here, it has passed structural Zod validation!
  const { username, message } = frame.payload;
  console.log(\`[\${username}]: \${message}\`);
});

// Automatic global validation error handler
server.on('validationError', (socket, error) => {
  console.error('Socket sent a bad payload:', error.issues);
  
  // AfterLink automatically replies with a MSG_ERROR (0x05) frame to the client
});
`;
---

<DocsLayout 
  title="Automatic Validation" 
  description="Learn how to define, register, and leverage automatic schema validation for AfterLink network frames."
>
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <div class="border-b border-al-border/50 pb-4">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-al-white mb-2">Automatic Schema Validation</h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-al-muted leading-relaxed">
        AfterLink integrates schema validation directly into the protocol frame processing layer. By leveraging <strong>Zod</strong> schemas, you can prevent corrupt, malformed, or hostile payloads from exhausting application layer computing cycles.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Security aspect -->
    <div class="bg-al-cyan/5 border border-al-cyan/20 rounded-lg p-6 my-6 select-all">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-xs font-bold text-al-cyan uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Protocol-Level Shield</h2>
      <p class="text-2xs text-al-text leading-relaxed">
        Typical websocket servers receive buffers, parse JSON string representations, and only then run validation in the handler. If a packet is malformed, your processor has already allocated CPU cycles and memory. AfterLink parses the 10-byte header length, parses the buffer, validates it at the decoder boundary, and rejects bad sockets immediately.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- How Validation works step-by-step -->
    <h2 id="validation-flow" class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-al-white mt-10 mb-3 select-none">Schema Registration</h2>
    <p class="text-xs text-al-muted leading-relaxed select-all">
      You register validation models for each of the specific message types on the server. Whenever a frame matching that type is received, the validation chain runs:
    </p>
    
    <div class="p-6 rounded-lg border border-al-border bg-al-card/25 font-mono text-3xs text-al-muted select-all">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <div class="flex justify-between items-center bg-al-black/40 p-2.5 rounded border border-al-border select-all">
          <span class="text-al-white">1. Frame Buffer Arrives</span>
          <span class="text-al-muted">Hex array stream parsed from socket</span>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center font-bold text-al-cyan select-none">&darr;</div>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-center bg-al-black/40 p-2.5 rounded border border-al-border select-all">
          <span class="text-al-white">2. Decode Header (10B)</span>
          <span class="text-al-muted">Check Magic (0xAF1C) and get Payload Length</span>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center font-bold text-al-cyan select-none">&darr;</div>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-center bg-al-cyan/5 p-2.5 rounded border border-al-cyan/20 select-all">
          <span class="text-al-cyan">3. Schema Check (Zod)</span>
          <span class="text-al-text">Runs pre-registered type validator against raw payload</span>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center font-bold text-al-cyan select-none">&darr;</div>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-center bg-al-black/40 p-2.5 rounded border border-al-border select-all">
          <span class="text-al-white">4. Handler Dispatch</span>
          <span class="text-al-muted">Emit 'message' with fully-typed Javascript object</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Code Validation Example -->
    <h2 id="validation-code" class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-al-white mt-12 mb-3 select-none">Validation Code Example</h2>
    <p class="text-xs text-al-muted leading-relaxed select-all">
      Below is an implementation of how schemas are registered and handled inside AfterLink server instances:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={validationExample} lang="javascript" filename="validation.js" />

    <!-- Error framing description -->
    <h2 id="error-framing" class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-al-white mt-12 mb-3 select-none">Error Frame (MSG_ERROR) Response</h2>
    <p class="text-xs text-al-muted leading-relaxed select-all">
      When a schema check fails, the socket connection is not closed immediately. Instead, AfterLink encodes a structured <code>MSG_ERROR</code> (<code>0x05</code>) frame back to the client. The payload of this error frame contains a JSON-serialized details block:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={`{
  "code": "BAD_PAYLOAD",
  "message": "Schema validation failed",
  "issues": [
    {
      "path": ["username"],
      "message": "String must contain at least 3 character(s)"
    }
  ]
}`} lang="json" filename="error-response.json" />

    <!-- Navigation links -->
    <div class="flex items-center justify-between border-t border-al-border/50 pt-8 mt-12 select-none">
      <a href="/docs/protocol" class="text-xs text-al-muted hover:text-al-cyan transition-colors font-mono">
        &larr; Protocol Layout
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/api" class="text-xs text-al-cyan hover:text-al-white transition-colors font-mono">
        Server & Client API &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
